Here's a C program that prints out "Madam" which is a palindrome:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Madam\n");
    return 0;
}
```

This program simply prints the string "Madam". However, if you want to create a function in C that checks whether a given string is a palindrome or not, here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int is_palindrome(const char *s) {
    int start = 0;
    int end = strlen(s) - 1;

    while (start < end) {
        if (*s == *s + strlen(s) - 1 - start) {
            start++;
        } else {
            return 0; // not a palindrome
        }
    }

    return 1; // it's a palindrome
}

int main() {
    const char *str = "radar";
    if (is_palindrome(str)) {
        printf("%s is a palindrome\n", str);
    } else {
        printf("%s is not a palindrome\n", str);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This function works by comparing characters from the start and end of the string, moving towards the center. If all pairs of characters match, it's a palindrome; otherwise, it's not.

It seems that the second C program I provided earlier has an error in the comparison inside the while loop.

Here's a corrected version of the `is_palindrome` function:

```c
int is_palindrome(const char *s) {
    int start = 0;
    int end = strlen(s) - 1;

    while (start < end) {
        if (*s == *(s + end - start)) { // Corrected comparison
            start++;
        } else {
            return 0; // not a palindrome
        }
    }

    return 1; // it's a palindrome
}
```

In the corrected version, I changed `*s == *s + strlen(s) - 1 - start` to `*(s + end - start)` to correctly compare characters from the start and end of the string. This should fix the issue with the second program not correctly identifying palindromes.
